<h1>Image Forgery Detection using a Deep Convolutional Neural Network</h1>
<p class="sub">Project Documentation &middot; CASIA dataset &middot; Binary classification (Authentic vs Spliced) &middot; MATLAB R2022b</p>

<h2>1. Introduction</h2>
<p>Digital images are now the primary medium through which information is shared, yet the same
editing tools that make images easy to create also make them easy to manipulate. <b>Image
splicing</b> &mdash; cutting a region from one photograph and pasting it into another &mdash; is
among the most common and most damaging forms of forgery, because a convincingly spliced image
can mislead viewers, distort evidence, and spread misinformation. The artefacts left behind by
splicing (irregular object boundaries, inconsistent sensor noise, and mismatched
colour-illumination) are often invisible to the human eye but are statistically detectable. This
makes the problem an excellent fit for <b>deep convolutional neural networks (DCNNs)</b>, which
learn discriminative features directly from raw pixels rather than relying on hand-crafted rules.</p>

<p>This project builds a complete, reproducible pipeline that detects spliced images in the
<b>CASIA forgery dataset</b>, treating the task as <b>binary classification</b> between two
classes: <code>Au</code> (authentic) and <code>Sp</code> (spliced). The work began from an
existing research script that trained a small custom network and reported roughly 98.5% accuracy.
On close inspection, that figure was found to be unreliable: the script validated the model on the
very images it had been trained on, so the number measured memorisation rather than the ability to
generalise to unseen images. The central goal of this documentation, and of the code it describes,
is to replace that flawed methodology with an honest, rigorously evaluated system, and to benchmark
several modern network architectures so that a trustworthy best model can be recommended.</p>

<h2>2. The Dataset and Pre-processing</h2>
<p>The dataset contains <b>10,919 colour images</b>, each resized to <b>114 &times; 114 &times; 3</b>
pixels and organised across eleven numbered folders, every one of which holds an <code>Au</code>
and an <code>Sp</code> subfolder. Class labels are inferred automatically from these folder names
using MATLAB's <code>imageDatastore</code>. Ten files in one folder are corrupt and crash the image
reader; a dedicated helper, <code>filterReadable.m</code>, performs a fast header check on every file
and silently discards the unreadable ones, leaving <b>10,909 usable images</b> &mdash; 5,598 authentic
and 5,311 spliced, a well balanced split.</p>

<p>The data is divided into <b>80% training and 20% validation</b> using a fixed random seed
(<code>rng(123)</code>). Fixing the seed is important: it guarantees that every model is trained and
tested on exactly the same partition, so differences in their scores reflect the models themselves and
not lucky or unlucky splits. Data augmentation is applied during training, but only <b>horizontal and
vertical flips together with small pixel translations</b>. Rotation and scaling are deliberately
excluded, because the interpolation they introduce smooths over the subtle, high-frequency cues that
distinguish a spliced region from an authentic one &mdash; aggressive geometric augmentation was found
to actively hurt performance.</p>

<h2>3. How the Code Works</h2>
<p>The pipeline is organised into two phases plus an aggregation step, all written as small, focused
MATLAB scripts that share a common set of evaluation helpers.</p>
<p><b>Phase 1 &mdash; testing the existing model.</b> <code>Test_Existing_Model.m</code> loads the
original trained network (<code>XONet_r003.mat</code>) and evaluates it <i>without retraining</i>. It
builds the datastore, applies the fixed split, classifies both the training and validation sets, and
produces the complete metric suite. This establishes the baseline.</p>
<p><b>Phase 2 &mdash; training new models.</b> <code>Train_Transfer.m</code> fine-tunes a pretrained
backbone, while <code>Train_Optimized.m</code> and <code>Train_CustomV3.m</code> train networks from
scratch for comparison. Crucially, these scripts capture the training-history structure returned by
<code>trainNetwork</code>, which the original code discarded. This history is what allows the pipeline
to plot genuine <b>training-versus-validation loss and accuracy curves</b>.</p>
<p><b>Aggregation.</b> <code>Compare_Models.m</code> scans every results folder, collects the saved
metrics, ranks the models by validation accuracy, and writes a unified comparison table
(<code>COMPARISON.csv</code> / <code>.txt</code>) and a bar chart. A PowerShell driver,
<code>run_queue.ps1</code>, automates the whole backbone sweep: it launches one fresh MATLAB process
per network, is resumable (it skips any model that already has a summary file), displays a live
progress bar, and finishes by refreshing the comparison automatically.</p>

<h2>4. Models and Methodology</h2>
<p>The heart of the project is <b>transfer learning</b>. A network pretrained on the large ImageNet
database already contains general-purpose visual feature detectors. Rather than learning these from
nothing, the pipeline takes such a backbone, <b>replaces its classification head</b> with a new layer
that outputs just two classes (configured with a tenfold learning-rate multiplier so it adapts quickly),
and fine-tunes the entire network on the forgery data. Five backbones were benchmarked:
<b>ResNet-18, ResNet-50, AlexNet, VGG-16, and SqueezeNet</b>. To demonstrate why pretraining matters,
three from-scratch convolutional networks were also trained; these plateaued near 77% accuracy because
the dataset is too small to learn strong low-level features from random initial weights.</p>

<h2>5. Training Configuration and Parameters</h2>
<p>The transfer-learning script is fully parameterised through workspace variables, so a new experiment
needs only a one-line command:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>BASENET</code></td><td>resnet18</td><td>Pretrained backbone to fine-tune</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>EPOCHS</code></td><td>12</td><td>Number of full passes over the training data</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>LR0</code></td><td>1e-4</td><td>Initial learning rate (Adam optimiser)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>MBS</code></td><td>64</td><td>Mini-batch size (reduced to 24 for VGG-16 on an 8&nbsp;GB GPU)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>TAG</code></td><td>=BASENET</td><td>Names the output results folder</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Training uses the Adam optimiser with a piecewise learning-rate schedule that halves the rate
periodically, an early-stopping validation patience, and a "best-validation-loss" checkpoint policy
that keeps the strongest network seen during training rather than simply the final one. All training
runs on the GPU via the automatic execution environment.</p>

<h2>6. Execution Workflow</h2>
<p>From the project directory, the typical sequence is: run Phase 1 to test the existing model; run the
backbone sweep with <code>run_queue.ps1</code> to train and evaluate every network; and finally run
<code>Compare_Models</code> to produce the ranked comparison. Each step writes its full console output
to a log file and saves all figures and metrics into a per-model results folder, so every experiment is
self-documenting and reproducible.</p>

<h2>7. Results and Comparison</h2>
<p>All numbers below are <b>honest, held-out validation results</b> on data the models never saw during
training. Training times were measured on an NVIDIA GTX 1070 GPU.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Model</th><th>Val Accuracy</th><th>Val F1</th><th>Val AUC</th><th>Training time</th></tr>
<tr class="best"><td>Transfer ResNet-50</td><td>0.9940</td><td>0.9940</td><td>0.9999</td><td>~1 h 18 m</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transfer ResNet-18</td><td>0.9881</td><td>0.9881</td><td>0.9996</td><td>~24 m</td></tr>
<tr><td>Existing XONet (baseline)*</td><td>0.9849</td><td>0.9849</td><td>0.9909</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transfer AlexNet</td><td>0.9748</td><td>0.9748</td><td>0.9975</td><td>~22 m</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transfer VGG-16</td><td>0.9533</td><td>0.9532</td><td>0.9928</td><td>~8 h 20 m</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transfer SqueezeNet (best)</td><td>0.9358</td><td>0.9358</td><td>0.9893</td><td>~18 m</td></tr>
<tr><td>From-scratch CNN (v1)</td><td>0.7741</td><td>0.7736</td><td>0.8678</td><td>~6 m</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="sub">* In-sample / optimistic baseline &mdash; included for reference only.</p>
<p>The best model is <b>Transfer ResNet-50</b>, reaching <b>99.40% validation accuracy</b> with an F1
score of 0.9940 and an AUC of 0.9999. The clear upward trend &mdash; from 77% for a from-scratch
network, to 93.6% for SqueezeNet, to 98.8% for ResNet-18, and finally 99.4% for ResNet-50 &mdash;
confirms that deeper pretrained backbones combined with forgery-safe augmentation are decisively
superior for this task. ResNet-50's residual connections let it train a deep network stably without
the long runtime cost of VGG-16, which took over eight hours yet scored markedly lower.</p>

<h2>8. Conclusion</h2>
<p>This project converts an unreliable, leakage-affected experiment into a transparent and reproducible
forgery-detection system. By enforcing a fixed train/validation split, capturing full training history,
benchmarking five architectures, and reporting only honest held-out metrics, it delivers a defensible
recommendation: a fine-tuned <b>ResNet-50</b> that detects spliced images with over 99% accuracy. All
code, results, logs, and the smaller trained models are version-controlled and documented, allowing any
result in this report to be regenerated end-to-end with a single command.</p>

</body>
